The genealogy of Jesus Christ
The Book of the Birth of Jesus Christ, son of David, son of Abraham:
Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
And Judah begot Perez and Zerah by Tamar. And Perez begot Hezron. And Hezron begot Ram.
Aram was the son of Aminadab. Aminadab was the son of Nahshon. Nahshon was the son of Salmon.
And Salmon was the father of Boaz by Rahab. And Boaz was the father of Obed by Ruth. And Obed was the father of Jesse.
And David the king begot Solomon. And Solomon the king begot from the wife of Uriah.
And Solomon was the son of Rehoboam. And Rehoboam was the son of Abijah. And Abijah was the son of Asa.
Asa begot Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat begot Joram. Joram begot Uzziah.
And Azariah begot Jotham. Jotham begot Ahaz. Ahaz begot Hezekiah.
And Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
And Josiah was born to Jeconiah and his brothers during the Babylonian exile.
And after the exile to Babylon, Kenaniah was born, and Shealtiel was born. And Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
And Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
And Azor was the son of Zadok. And Zadok was the son of Achim. And Achim was the son of Eliud.
Eliud was the father of Eleazar. Eleazar was the father of Matthan. Matthan was the father of Jacob.
And Jacob was the father of Joseph, the husband of Mary, of whom was born Jesus, who is called the Christ.
So all the generations from Abraham to David are fourteen generations, and from David to the Babylonian exile are fourteen generations, and from the Babylonian exile to the Christ are fourteen generations. The birth of Jesus Christ
The birth of Jesus Christ was as follows: When his mother Mary was betrothed to Joseph, before they came together, she was found to be pregnant through the Holy Spirit.
Yusuf divorced her as he was righteous, and he did not want to make it public; he wanted to dismiss her secretly.
But while he was pondering these things, the angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream, saying: "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife. For the one conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit.
She will give birth to a son and call his name Jesus, because he will save his people from their sins.
And all this was to fulfill what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet who said:
"Behold, the virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and his name shall be called Emmanuel," which means, God with us.
When Joseph woke up from sleep, he did as the angel of the Lord commanded him, and took his wife.
She did not know him until she gave birth to her firstborn son. And she named him Jesus.
Visit of the Magi
When Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea, in the days of King Herod, behold, wise men from the East came to Jerusalem.
Saying, "Where is the newborn king of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the East and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was disturbed, and all Jerusalem with him.
So he gathered all the chief priests and the scribes of the people and asked them, "Where is the Christ to be born?"
So they said to him: "In Bethlehem of Judea. For thus it is written by the prophet:
And you, Bethlehem, land of Judah, are by no means least among the rulers of Judah, for from you shall come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.
Then Herod secretly called the Magi and inquired carefully about the time the star had appeared.
Then he sent them to Bethlehem and said, "Go and search carefully for the child. And when you find him, let me know, so that I too may come and worship him."
When they heard from the king, they went. And behold, the star that they had seen in the east went before them until it came and stood over where the child was.
When they saw the star, they rejoiced with great joy.
They came to the house and saw the child with his mother Mary. They bowed down and worshiped him. Then they opened their treasures and presented him with gifts: gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
Then, when they were divinely inspired in a dream not to return to Herod, they departed by another route to their country. The flight to Egypt
And after they had gone, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream, saying: "Rise, take the child and his mother, and flee to Egypt, and stay there until I tell you. For Herod is about to search for the child to destroy him."
So he arose and took the boy and his mother by night and departed to Egypt.
And this was to fulfill what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet, saying: "Out of Egypt I called my son."
Then when Herod saw that the Magi had deceived him, he was very angry. He sent and killed all the boys in Bethlehem and in all its surroundings, from two years old and under, according to the time he had ascertained from the Magi.
Then what was said by the prophet Jeremiah was fulfilled:
A voice was heard in Ramah, lamentation, weeping, and great mourning. Rachel is weeping for her children and refuses to be comforted, because they are no more. Return to Nazareth
When Herod died, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream in Egypt.
Saying: "Arise, take the child and his mother, and go to the land of Israel, for those who sought the child's life are dead."
So he got up, took the boy and his mother, and came to the land of Israel.
But when he heard that Archelaus was reigning over Judea instead of his father Herod, he was afraid to go there. And being warned in a dream, he withdrew to the regions of Galilee.
And he came and lived in a city called Nazareth, so that what was spoken by the prophets might be fulfilled: "He shall be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist paves the way
And in those days John the Baptist came preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
Saying: "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has come near."
For this is he of whom Isaiah the prophet spoke, saying: A voice of one crying in the wilderness: Prepare the way of the Lord. Make his paths straight.
And this John had his garment made of camel's hair, and a leather belt around his waist; and his food was locusts and wild honey.
Then Jerusalem went out to him, and all Judea, and all the region around the Jordan,
And they relied on it in Jordan, acknowledging their sins.
When he saw many of the Pharisees and Sadducees coming to his baptism, he said to them, "You brood of vipers, who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?
So produce fruits worthy of repentance.
And do not think that you can say to yourselves, "We have Abraham as an ancestor." For I say to you, God is able to raise up children for Abraham from these stones.
And now the axe is laid to the root of the tree, so every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water for repentance, but the one who comes after me is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to carry. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
He who winnows with his hand, and will thoroughly cleanse his threshing floor, and will gather his wheat into the barn; but the chaff he will burn with unquenchable fire." Baptism of Jesus Christ
Then Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan to John, to be baptized by him.
But John forbade him, saying, "I need to be baptized by you, and you come to me!"
Jesus answered him, "Allow it now, for this is fitting for us to fulfill all righteousness." Then he allowed him.
When Jesus had been baptized, he immediately came up out of the water, and behold, the heavens were opened to him, and he saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove and coming upon him,
And a voice from the heavens said, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
Jesus Faces the Temptation
Then Jesus was led by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil.
After fasting for forty days and forty nights, he finally became hungry.
So the tempted one approached him and said to him, "If you are the Son of God, tell these stones to become bread."
He answered and said: "It is written: Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God."
Then Satan took him to the holy city and stood him on the wing of the temple,
And he said to him: "If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down, for it is written: He will command his angels concerning you, to guard you in all your ways; they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone."
Jesus said to him, "It is also written: You shall not put the Lord your God to the test."
Then Satan took him to a very high mountain and showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory,
And he said to him: "I will give you all of this if you fall down and worship me."
Then Jesus said to him, "Go away, Satan! For it is written: You shall worship the Lord your God, and him only shall you serve."
Then Satan left him, and angels came and began to serve him. The beginning of his ministry in Galilee
When Jesus heard that John had been handed over, he withdrew to Galilee.
And he left Nazareth and came to live in Capernaum, which is by the sea, in the territory of Zebulun and Naphtali,
So that what was said by the prophet Isaiah who said:
Land of Zebulun, and land of Naphtali, the way of the sea, beyond the Jordan, Galilee of the nations.
The people sitting in darkness have seen a great light, and those sitting in the region of death and its shadows, a light has shone upon them.
From that time Jesus began to preach and say: "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has come near." The calling of the first disciples
And as Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee, he saw two brothers: Simon, who is called Peter, and Andrew his brother, casting a net into the sea, for they were fishermen.
So he said to them, "Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
For the time being, he left the window and followed him.
Then he passed from there and saw two other brothers: James son of Zebedee and John his brother, in the boat with their father Zebedee, mending their nets, so he called them.
At that time, they left the boat and their father and followed him. Jesus teaches and heals the sick.
And Jesus went throughout all Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every disease and every sickness among the people.
His reputation spread throughout all of Syria. They brought to him all the sick afflicted with various diseases and pains, the insane, the epileptics, and the paralyzed, and he healed them.
And large crowds from Galilee, the Decapolis, Jerusalem, Judea, and from beyond the Jordan followed him.
The Sermon on the Mount - The Beatitudes
And when the crowds saw him, he went up on the mountain, and when he sat down, his disciples came to him.
Then he opened his mouth and taught them, saying:
Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be satisfied.
Blessed are the merciful, for they shall receive mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called children of God.
Blessed are those who are persecuted for righteousness' sake, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when they insult you, persecute you, and say all kinds of evil against you falsely because of me.
Rejoice and be glad, for your reward is great in heaven, for in the same way they persecuted the prophets who were before you. Salt of the earth and light of the world
You are the salt of the earth; but if the salt loses its saltiness, how can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, except to be thrown out and trampled underfoot by men.
You are the light of the world. A city set on a hill cannot be hidden,
They do not light a lamp and put it under a measuring basket, but on the lampstand, and it gives light to all who are in the house.
Let your light shine before others, that they may see your good deeds and glorify your Father in heaven. Completion of the Law
"Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish them but to fulfill them."
Truly, I say to you: until heaven and earth pass away, not one letter or one stroke shall pass from the law until all is fulfilled.
For whoever breaks one of these lesser commandments and teaches people so, shall be called least in the kingdom of heaven. But whoever does and teaches, this one shall be called great in the kingdom of heaven.
For I tell you, unless your righteousness exceeds that of the scribes and Pharisees, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven. Anger
You have heard that it was said to the ancients: Do not kill, and whoever kills shall be subject to judgment.
But I say to you: Whoever is angry with his brother without cause shall be liable to judgment, and whoever says to his brother, 'Raca,' shall be liable to the council, and whoever says, 'You fool,' shall be liable to the fire of hell.
If you bring your offering to the altar, and there remember that your brother has something against you,
So leave your offering there before the altar, and first go and be reconciled with your brother; then come and offer your gift.
Be quick to reconcile with your opponent while you are on the way with him, lest your opponent hand you over to the judge, and the judge hands you over to the police, and you end up in prison.
The truth I tell you: Do not leave there until you pay the last penny! Adultery
You have heard that it was said to those of old, "You shall not commit adultery."
But I say to you: Everyone who looks at a woman with lust has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to stumble, pluck it out and cast it away from you; for it is better for you that one of your members perish than for your whole body to be cast into hell.
And if your right hand causes you to stumble, cut it off and cast it away from you; for it is better for you that one of your members perish than for your whole body to be cast into Hell. Divorce
It was said: Whoever divorces his wife should give her a written divorce certificate.
As for me, I say to you: Whoever divorces his wife except for the cause of fornication makes her commit fornication, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits fornication. The oath.
You have also heard that it was said to those of old, "You shall not swear falsely, but shall perform to the Lord what you have sworn."
As for me, I say to you: Do not swear at all, neither by heaven, for it is the throne of God,
Nor by the earth, for it is the place of His feet, nor by Jerusalem, for it is the city of the great King.
And do not swear by your head, for you cannot make one hair white or black.
But let your word be: Yes, yes; no, no. Anything beyond this is from the evil one. Revenge
You have heard that it was said: an eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth.
But I say to you: Do not resist evil, but if anyone strikes you on the right cheek, turn to him the other also.
And whoever wants to sue you and take your shirt, let him have your cloak as well.
If someone compels you to go one mile, go with him two.
Whoever asks you, give to him; and whoever wants to borrow from you, do not turn him away. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said: Love your neighbor and hate your enemy.
But I say to you: Love your enemies. Bless those who curse you. Do good to those who hate you, and pray for those who mistreat you and persecute you,
So that you may be children of your Father in heaven; for He causes His sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous.
For if you love those who love you, what reward do you have? Do not even the tax collectors do the same?
And if you greet only your brothers, what special merit is there in that? Do not even the tax collectors do the same?
Be perfect, therefore, as your Father in heaven is perfect.
Charity
Beware of practicing your righteousness before others to be seen by them, otherwise you will have no reward from your Father who is in heaven.
So when you give charity, do not sound a trumpet before you, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and in the streets, to be praised by others. Truly I tell you, they have received their reward!
But when you give charity, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing,
So that your charity may be in secret. For your Father who sees in secret will reward you openly. Prayer
And when you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and on the street corners to be seen by people. Truly I tell you, they have received their reward!
But when you pray, go into your room, close your door, and pray to your Father who is in secret. And your Father who sees in secret will reward you openly.
And when you pray, do not repeat the words vainly as the nations do, for they think that by their many words they will be heard.
So do not be like them. For your Father knows what you need before you ask Him.
Pray like this: Our Father in heaven, hallowed be your name.
Let your kingdom come. Let your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us today our daily bread.
And forgive us our sins as we also forgive those who sin against us.
And do not lead us into temptation, but deliver us from the evil one. For yours is the kingdom, and the power, and the glory, forever. Amen.
If you forgive people their faults, your Heavenly Father will also forgive you.
And if you do not forgive people their mistakes, your Father will not forgive your mistakes either. Fasting
And when you fast, do not be gloomy like the hypocrites, for they disfigure their faces to show people they are fasting. Truly I tell you, they have received their reward.
But when you fast, anoint your head and wash your face,
So that you do not appear to people to be fasting, but to your Father who is in secret. And your Father who sees in secret will reward you openly. Treasures in heaven
Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
But store up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, and where thieves do not break in or steal,
For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.
The lamp of the body is the eye; if your eye is simple, your whole body will be full of light.
And if your eye is evil, your whole body will be full of darkness. If the light within you is darkness, how great is that darkness!
No one can serve two masters, because they will either hate one and love the other, or be devoted to one and despise the other. You cannot serve both God and money. God takes care of us.
Therefore I tell you: Do not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink, nor about your body, what you will wear. Is not life more than food, and the body more than clothing?
Look at the birds of the sky: they do not sow or reap or gather into barns, yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much more valuable than they?
And which of you, if he is concerned, can add one cubit to his stature?
And why do you worry about clothing? Consider the lilies of the field, how they grow! They do not toil nor spin.
But I say to you: not even Solomon in all his glory was dressed like one of these.
If the grass of the field that exists today and is thrown into the oven tomorrow is clothed by God like this, is it not much more fitting that He clothes you, O you of little faith?
So do not worry, saying: What shall we eat? Or what shall we drink? Or what shall we wear?
For all these things the nations of the world eagerly seek, for your heavenly Father knows that you need all these things.
But seek first the kingdom of God and His righteousness, and all these things will be added to you.
So do not worry about tomorrow, for tomorrow will worry about itself. Today has enough trouble of its own.
Condemning others
"Do not judge, so that you will not be judged,"
For with the judgment you pronounce you will be judged, and with the measure you use it will be measured to you.
And why do you look at the speck of sawdust in your brother’s eye, but do not notice the plank in your own eye?
Or how can you say to your brother, "Let me take the speck out of your eye," while there is a plank in your own eye?
O hypocrite, first take the plank out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take the speck out of your brother's eye!
Do not give what is holy to the dogs, nor cast your pearls before swine, lest they trample them under their feet and turn to tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, knock.
Ask and you shall receive. Seek and you shall find. Knock and it will be opened to you.
For everyone who asks receives, and the one who seeks finds, and to the one who knocks it will be opened.
Or which one of you, if his son asks him for bread, will give him a stone?
If a fish asks him, does he give it a snake?
If you, being evil, know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more will your Father in heaven give good things to those who ask Him!
So in everything, do to others what you would have them do to you, for this sums up the Law and the Prophets. The narrow gate
Enter through the narrow gate, for wide is the gate and broad is the road that leads to destruction, and many enter through it!
How narrow is the gate and difficult the road that leads to life, and few are those who find it! The tree and its fruit
Beware of false prophets who come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravenous wolves!
By their fruits you shall know them. Do men gather grapes from thornbushes, or figs from thistles?
Thus, every good tree produces good fruit, but the bad tree produces bad fruit,
A good tree cannot produce bad fruit, nor can a bad tree produce good fruit.
Every tree that does not produce good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
So by their fruits you shall know them.
Not everyone who says to me, "Lord, Lord," will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day: Lord, Lord! Did we not prophesy in your name, and in your name cast out demons, and in your name perform many miracles?
Then I make it clear to them: I have never known you! Depart from me, you workers of iniquity! The wise builders and the ignorant builders
"Therefore, everyone who hears these words of mine and acts on them is like a wise man who built his house on the rock."
Then the rain fell, the rivers came, the winds blew, and they struck that house, but it did not fall, because it was founded on the rock.
And everyone who hears these words of mine and does not act on them is like a foolish man who built his house on the sand.
Then the rain fell, the rivers came, the winds blew, and they struck that house, so it fell, and its fall was great!».
When Jesus had finished these sayings, the crowds were amazed at his teaching,
Because he taught them as one who had authority, and not as the scribes.
Healing of a Leper
And when he came down from the mountain, large crowds followed him.
And if a leper came and prostrated before him saying: "O Master, if you wish, you can make me clean."
Then Jesus reached out his hand and touched him, saying, "I am willing; be clean!" Immediately his leprosy was cleansed.
Then Jesus said to him, "See that you say nothing to anyone. But go, show yourself to the priest, and offer the sacrifice that Moses commanded, as a testimony to them." Faith of the centurion
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a centurion came to him, asking for help.
And he says: "O sir, my servant is lying in the house, paralyzed and in great pain."
Then Jesus said to him, "I am coming to heal him."
The commander of the hundred answered and said, "Lord, I am not worthy that you should enter under my roof, but just say the word, and my servant will be healed."
Because I am also a human under authority. I have soldiers under my command. I say to this one: Go! He goes, and to another: Come! He comes, and to my servant: Do this! He does.
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed and said to those following him, "Truly I tell you, I have not found anyone in Israel with faith like this!"
And I say to you: Many will come from the east and the west and will recline with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob in the kingdom of the heavens,
As for the children of the kingdom, they are cast into the outer darkness. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth there.
Then Jesus said to the centurion, "Go, and as you have believed, let it be done for you." And his servant was healed at that very hour. Healing of Peter's mother-in-law and others
When Jesus came to Peter's house, he saw his mother-in-law lying down and feverish,
He touched her hand, so the fever left her; she got up and served them.
And when evening came, many lunatics were brought to him; he cast out the spirits with a word, and healed all who were sick,
So that what was said by the prophet Isaiah, who said: "He took our infirmities and bore our diseases," may be fulfilled. The price of discipleship
When Jesus saw the large crowds around him, he commanded to go to the other side.
A writer stepped forward and said to him: "O teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
And Jesus said to him, "Foxes have holes and birds of the sky have nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head."
And another of his disciples said to him, "Lord, permit me first to go and bury my father."
Then Jesus said to him, "Follow me, and let the dead bury their own dead." Calming the storm
And when he entered the ship, his disciples followed him.
And a great disturbance occurred in the sea until the waves covered the ship, and he was sleeping.
His disciples came to him and woke him, saying, "Lord, save us, for we are perishing!"
He said to them, "Why are you afraid, O you of little faith?" Then he got up and rebuked the winds and the sea, and there was a great calm.
So the people were amazed, saying: "What kind of man is this? For the winds and the sea all obey him!" Expelling the demons
And when he came to the region of the Gergesenes, he was met by two madmen coming out of the tombs, very violent, so that no one could pass that way.
And they both cried out, saying, "What have we to do with you, Jesus, Son of God? Have you come here before the time to torment us?"
And far from them was a large herd of pigs grazing.
So the devils asked him, saying: "If you cast us out, permit us to go to the herd of swine."
So he said to them, "Go." And they went away and drove the herd of pigs into the sea, and the whole herd of pigs rushed down the steep bank into the sea and drowned in the water.
As for the shepherds, they fled and went to the city, and reported everything, including the matter of the madmen.
So the whole city went out to meet Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged him to leave their region.
Healing of a Paralyzed Person
So he entered the ship, crossed over, and came to his city.
And when they brought the paralyzed man to Him, lying on a bed, Jesus saw their faith and said to the paralyzed man, "Take heart, son; your sins are forgiven."
And indeed, a group of the scribes said about themselves, "This is blaspheming!"
Jesus knew their thoughts and said, "Why do you think evil in your hearts?"
Which is easier to say: Your sins are forgiven, or get up and walk?
But so that you may know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then he said to the paralyzed man, "Get up, take your mat and go home!"
He got up and went to his house.
When the crowds saw it, they were amazed and glorified God, who had given such authority to men. The Gospel of Matthew
As Jesus was passing by, he saw a man sitting at the tax collector's booth, named Matthew. He said to him, "Follow me." So he got up and followed him.
And while he was reclining in the house, many tax collectors and sinners came and reclined with Jesus and his disciples.
When the Pharisees saw this, they said to his disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
When Jesus heard it, he said to them, "It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick."
So go and learn what this means: I desire mercy, not sacrifice, for I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance." The question about fasting
Then the disciples of John came to him, saying, "Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but your disciples do not fast?"
Then Jesus said to them, "Can the wedding guests mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? But the days will come when the bridegroom will be taken away from them, and then they will fast.
No one patches an old garment with a piece of new cloth, for the fullness takes from the garment, making the tear worse.
And they do not put new wine into old wineskins, lest the wineskins burst; for the wine will burst and the wineskins will be ruined. But they put new wine into new wineskins, and both are preserved.» The raising of Jairus's daughter and the healing of the woman with the issue of blood
While he was speaking to them, a ruler came and bowed down before him, saying, "My daughter has just died, but come and lay your hand on her, and she will live."
Then Jesus got up and he and his disciples followed him.
And a woman who had been bleeding for twelve years came up behind him and touched the fringe of his garment,
Because she said to herself: "If I only touch his garment, I will be healed."
Jesus turned and saw her, and said, "Take heart, daughter; your faith has healed you." And the woman was healed from that hour.
And when Jesus came to the chief's house, and saw the psalmists and the crowd making a noise,
He said to them: "Step aside, for the girl is not dead but asleep." They laughed at him.
When the group came out, he entered and held her hand, so the girl stood up.
So that news spread throughout all that land. Healing of two blind men
And as Jesus passed by from there, two blind men followed him, crying out and saying, "Have mercy on us, Son of David!"
And when he came to the house, the two blind men came to him, and Jesus said to them, "Do you believe that I am able to do this?" They said to him, "Yes, Lord!"
Then he touched their eyes, saying, "According to your faith, let it be done to you."
Then their eyes were opened. Jesus rebuked them, saying, "Look, let no one know!"
But they went out and spread it throughout all that land. Healing the mute.
And while they were going out, a mute madman was brought to him.
When the devil was cast out, the mute man spoke, and the crowds were amazed, saying, "Nothing like this has ever been seen in Israel!"
But the Pharisees said, "By the prince of demons he casts out demons!" The deed is few.
And Jesus was going through all the towns and villages, teaching in their synagogues, proclaiming the good news of the kingdom, and healing every disease and every sickness among the people.
And when the crowds saw, he took pity on them, for they were distressed and scattered like sheep without a shepherd.
Then he said to his disciples, "The harvest is plentiful, but the workers are few."
So ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest.
Sending the Twelve
Then he called his twelve disciples and gave them authority over unclean spirits to cast them out, and to heal every disease and every weakness.
As for the names of the twelve apostles, they are these: first, Simon who is called Peter, and Andrew his brother. James son of Zebedee, and John his brother.
Philips, and Bartholomew. Thomas, and Matthew the tax collector. James son of Alphaeus, and Lebbaeus called Thaddaeus.
Simon the Zealot, and Judas Iscariot, who betrayed him.
These twelve Jesus sent out and commanded them, saying: "Do not go on the way of the Gentiles, and do not enter a city of the Samaritans.
But go freely to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.
And as you go, preach, saying: The kingdom of heaven has come near.
Heal the sick. Cleanse the lepers. Raise the dead. Cast out demons. Freely you have received, freely give.
Do not acquire gold, silver, or copper in your regions,
Neither a bag for the road, nor two garments, nor shoes, nor a staff, because the doer is deserving of his food.
And any city or village you enter, inquire who in it is worthy, and stay there until you depart.
And when you enter the house, greet it with peace,
If the house is deserving, let your peace come upon it; but if it is not deserving, then let your peace return to you.
And whoever does not accept you nor listen to your words, then leave that house or that city, and shake off the dust from your feet.
Truly I tell you, the day of judgment will be more bearable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
Here I send you out like sheep among wolves; therefore be as shrewd as snakes and as innocent as doves.
But beware of the people, for they will hand you over to councils, and in their synagogues they will flog you.
And you will be brought before governors and kings for my sake as a testimony to them and to the nations.
So when they deliver you, do not worry about how or what you speak, because at that moment you will give what you speak.
For it is not you who speak, but the Spirit of your Father who speaks in you.
And brother will deliver up brother to death, and a father his child; and children will rise up against their parents and kill them,
And you will be hated by all for my name's sake. But the one who endures to the end will be saved.
And when they persecute you in this city, flee to another. Truly I tell you, you will not finish going through the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is not better than the teacher, nor is the servant better than his master.
It is enough for the student to be like his teacher, and the servant like his master. If they have called the lord of the house Beelzebub, how much more so the members of his household!
So do not fear them. For nothing hidden will remain undisclosed, nor anything concealed will remain unknown.
What I tell you in the dark, say in the light, and what you hear in the ear, proclaim it on the rooftops,
And do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul; rather fear Him who can destroy both soul and body in hell.
Are not two sparrows sold for a penny? Yet not one of them will fall to the ground without your Father's will.
As for you, even the hairs of your heads are all counted.
So do not be afraid! You are better than many sparrows!
Whoever acknowledges me before others, I also will acknowledge before my Father in heaven,
But whoever denies me before others, I also will deny before my Father who is in heaven.
Do not think that I have come to bring peace on earth. I have not come to bring peace, but a sword.
For I have come to set a man against his father, a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law.
And the enemies of a person are his own household.
Whoever loves a father or a mother more than me does not deserve me, and whoever loves a son or a daughter more than me does not deserve me,
And whoever does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever finds their life will lose it, and whoever loses their life for my sake will find it.
Whoever accepts you accepts me, and whoever accepts me accepts the one who sent me.
Whoever accepts a prophet in the name of a prophet will receive the reward of a prophet, and whoever accepts a righteous person in the name of a righteous person will receive the reward of a righteous person.
And whoever gives one of these little ones even a cup of cold water in the name of a disciple, truly I tell you, he will not lose his reward.
John the Baptist and Jesus
And when Jesus had finished instructing his twelve disciples, he departed from there to teach and preach in their cities. Jesus and John the Baptist
As for John, when he heard in prison about the works of the Messiah, he sent two of his disciples,
And he said to him: "Are you the one who is to come, or shall we wait for another?"
And Jesus answered them, "Go and tell John what you hear and see:"
The blind see, the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead rise, and the poor are glad.
Blessed is the one who does not stumble in it.
And while these two went, Jesus began to say to the crowds about John: "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
But what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? Behold, those who wear fine clothes are in kings' houses.
But what did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and better than a prophet.
For this is the one of whom it is written: Behold, I send my messenger before your face, who will prepare your way before you.
Truly I tell you: Among those born of women there has not risen anyone greater than John the Baptist, but the least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.
From the days of John the Baptist until now, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing, and forceful men lay hold of it.
Because all the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
And if you are willing to accept, this is Elijah who is to come.
Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear.
And to whom is this generation similar? It is like children sitting in the markets calling out to their companions.
And they say: Our groups came to you, but you did not dance! We came to you, but you did not beat your chests!
Because John came neither eating nor drinking, they say: he has a demon.
The Son of Man came eating and drinking, and they say, "Here is a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners." But wisdom is justified by her children. Woe to the cities that did not repent.
Then He began to rebuke the cities where most of His forces had been made because they did not repent:
Woe to you, Korzin! Woe to you, house of Sidon! For if the forces made in Tyre and Sidon were made in you, they would have long ago repented in the ointments and ashes.
But I say to you: that images and hunting dogs will have a more likely case on the Day of Judgment than you two.
And you, Capernaum, exalted to heaven! You will be brought down to the grave. For if the miracles done in you had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.
But I tell you: the land of Sodom will have a more likely state on the Day of Judgment than you. Rest for the weary.
At that time Jesus answered and said: "I praise you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and the intelligent and have revealed them to children.
Yes, Father, for so it has pleased you.
All that the Father has given me will come to me, and no one knows the Son except the Father, nor does anyone know the Father except the Son and those to whom the Son chooses to reveal him.
Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Take my yoke upon you and learn from me, for I am gentle and humble in heart, and you will find rest for your souls.
Because my nerves are thin and my burden is light.
Lord of Saturday
At that time Jesus went through the grainfields on the Sabbath, and his disciples began to pick some heads of grain and eat them.
So the Pharisees, when they saw it, said to him, "Look, your disciples are doing what is not lawful to do on the Sabbath!"
So he said to them: "Have you not read what David did when he and those with him were hungry?
How he entered the house of God and ate the bread of the offering, which it was not lawful for him or those with him to eat, but only for the priests.
Or what you read in the Torah that the priests on the Sabbath in the temple desecrate the Sabbath while they are innocent?
But I say to you: Here is greater than the temple!
If you knew what it is: I desire mercy, not sacrifice, you would not have condemned the innocent!
"For the Son of Man is also Lord of the Sabbath."
Then he departed from there and came to their assembly,
And if a man’s hand is dry, they asked him, saying: "Is it permissible to perform ablution with the leather socks?" to accuse him.
He said to them: "If any one of you has a sheep, and it falls into a pit on the Sabbath, will he not hold it and lift it out?
So how much better is a human than a sheep! If only the act of goodness would take place on Saturday!».
Then He said to the man: "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out. And it returned healthy like the other. The chosen servant of God
When the Pharisees went out, they conspired against him to put him to death,
Then Jesus knew and withdrew from there. And large crowds followed him, and he healed them all.
And he advised them not to reveal him,
So that what was said by the prophet Isaiah who said:
Behold my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved in whom my soul delights. I will put my spirit upon him, and he will proclaim justice to the nations.
He does not argue or shout, and no one hears his voice in the streets.
A crushed reed does not break, and a smoking wick is not extinguished, until the truth emerges victorious.
"And in His name shall the nations hope." Jesus and Beelzebub
Then he brought to him a madman who was blind and mute, and healed him, so that the blind mute spoke and saw.
All the crowds were amazed and said, "Could this be the Son of David?"
As for the Pharisees, when they heard it, they said, "This man does not cast out demons except by Beelzebul, the prince of demons."
Jesus knew their thoughts and said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation, and every city or house divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, then he is divided against himself. How then can his kingdom stand?
And if I cast out demons by Beelzebul, by whom do your sons cast them out? Therefore, they will be your judges!
But if I cast out demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you!
Or how can someone enter the house of the strong man and plunder his goods, unless he first binds the strong man, and then he will plunder his house?
Who is not with me is against me, and who does not gather with me scatters.
Therefore I tell you: every sin and blasphemy will be forgiven people, but blasphemy against the Spirit will not be forgiven people.
And whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, neither in this world nor in the one to come.
Make the tree good and its fruit good, or make the tree bad and its fruit bad, for by the fruit the tree is known.
O children of vipers! How can you speak good things while you are evil? For out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks.
The righteous person is like a good treasure in the heart from which good deeds emerge, and the wicked person is like a bad treasure from which evils emerge.
But I tell you: every idle word that people speak, they will give account for it on the Day of Judgment.
"Because with your words you justify yourself, and with your words you are condemned." Ayah Younan
Then some of the scribes and Pharisees answered, saying, "Teacher, we want to see a sign from you."
He answered and said to them: "A wicked and adulterous generation seeks a sign, and no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah the prophet."
For as Jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the whale, so the Son of Man will be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.
The men of Nineveh will rise up at the judgment with this generation and condemn it, because they repented at Jonah's preaching, and behold, something greater than Jonah is here!
The Queen of Sheba will come in faith with this generation and believe, for she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and here is one greater than Solomon!
When the impure spirit leaves the person, it passes through places where there is no water, seeking rest but finds none.
Then he says: I will return to my house from which I came out. So he comes and finds it empty, swept, and decorated.
Then he goes and takes with him seven other spirits more evil than himself, and they enter and dwell there, so that the last state of that person is worse than the first! This is also how it will be for this evil generation." The will of God is done.
And while he was speaking to the crowds, behold, his mother and brothers stood outside, seeking to speak to him.
And someone said to him, "Here are your mother and your brothers standing outside, wanting to speak to you."
He answered and said to the one who asked him: "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Then he stretched out his hand toward his disciples and said, "Here are my mother and my brothers."
"For whoever does the will of my Father in heaven is my brother and sister and mother."
Like the farmer and its interpretation
On that day Jesus went out of the house and sat by the sea,
So a great crowd gathered to him, until he entered the ship and sat down. And the whole crowd stood on the shore.
So he spoke to them many things in parables, saying: "Behold, the sower went out to sow,"
And while he was sowing, some fell on the path, and the birds came and ate it.
And the last fell on the rocky places, where there was not much soil, and it immediately sprang up because it had no depth of earth.
But when the sun rose, it burned, and since it had no root, it withered.
And another fell among thorns, and the thorns grew up and choked it.
And another fell on good ground and yielded fruit, some a hundred, another sixty, and another thirty.
Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear.
Then the disciples came and said to him, "Why do you speak to them in parables?"
And he answered and said to them, "Because it has been given to you to know the secrets of the kingdom of the heavens, but to those it has not been given."
For whoever has will be given more, and whoever does not have, even what they have will be taken from them.
For this reason I speak to them in parables, because seeing they do not see, and hearing they do not hear nor understand.
The prophecy of Isaiah has been fulfilled in them, saying: You will hear but never understand, and you will see but never perceive.
Because the heart of this people has grown dull, and their ears are heavy of hearing. They have closed their eyes, lest they should see with their eyes, hear with their ears, understand with their hearts, and turn again, and I should heal them.
But blessed are your eyes because they see, and your ears because they hear.
Truly, I say to you: Many prophets and righteous people longed to see what you see but did not see it, and to hear what you hear but did not hear it.
So listen, you are like the sower:
Everyone who hears the word of the kingdom and does not understand it, the evil one comes and snatches away what was sown in their heart. This is the one sown along the path.
And the one sown on rocky places is the one who hears the word and immediately receives it with joy,
But it has no root in itself; rather, it is temporary. So if hardship or persecution occurs because of the word, it immediately stumbles.
And the seed sown among thorns is the one who hears the word, but the worries of this world and the deceitfulness of wealth choke the word, making it unfruitful.
As for what was sown on good soil, it is the one who hears the word and understands it. He is the one who produces fruit, some a hundred, some sixty, and some thirty. The parable of the wheat and the tares.
He gave them another example, saying: "The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seed in his field.
While the people were asleep, his enemy came and sowed weeds among the wheat and went away.
When the plants sprouted and produced fruit, then the thorns also appeared.
Then the servant of the house came and said to him, "O master, did you not sow good seed in your field? So where did this tares come from?"
So he said to them: A human enemy did this. The servants said to him: Do you want us to go and gather him?
He said: No! Lest you uproot the wheat along with the weeds while you are gathering it.
Let them both grow together until the harvest; and at the time of the harvest I will say to the harvesters: First gather the weeds and bind them in bundles to burn them, but gather the wheat into my barn.” The parable of the mustard seed and the parable of the yeast.
He gave them another example, saying: "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that a man took and planted in his field,
It is the smallest of all seeds. But when it grows, it becomes the largest of all plants, and becomes a tree, so much so that the birds of the sky come and take shelter in its branches.
He told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast that a woman took and hid in three measures of flour until it was all leavened."
All this Jesus spoke to the crowds in parables, and without a parable he did not speak to them.
For what was said by the Prophet who said: "I will open my mouth with parables, and speak of what has been hidden since the foundation of the world." Interpretation of the parable of wheat and tares
Then Jesus dismissed the crowds and went into the house. His disciples came to him and said, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered and said to them: "The sower of the good seed is the Son of Man.
And the field is the world. And the good seed are the children of the kingdom. And the tares are the children of the wicked one.
And the enemy who sowed it is Satan. And the harvest is the end of the world. And the harvesters are the angels.
Just as the moth gathers and burns in the fire, so it will be at the end of this world:
The Son of Man will send his angels, and they will gather out of his kingdom all causes of sin and all law-breakers,
And they will be thrown into the blazing fire. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
Then the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear. Examples of the treasure, the pearl, and the net
The kingdom of heaven is also like a treasure hidden in a field, which a man found and hid. And in his joy, he went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
The kingdom of heaven is also like a merchant seeking fine pearls,
When he found one pearl of great value, he went and sold all that he had and bought it.
The kingdom of heaven is also like a net thrown into the sea, gathering fish of every kind.
When it was filled, they lifted it onto the shore, sat down, and gathered the horses into containers, while the cloaks were thrown outside.
This is how it will be at the end of the world: the angels will come out and separate the wicked from among the righteous,
And they will be thrown into the blazing fire. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
Jesus said to them, "Do you understand all this?" They answered, "Yes, Lord."
He said to them: "Therefore, every scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a house who brings out of his treasure new things and old."
And when Jesus had finished these parables, he moved on from there. Nazareth rejects Jesus
And when he came to his homeland, he taught them in their assembly until they were amazed and said, "Where did this wisdom and these powers come from?"
Isn't this the carpenter's son? Isn't his mother called Mary, and his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
"Are not all his sisters with us? So where did he get all this from?"
They were offended by him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country and in his own house."
And many forces were not made there due to their lack of faith.
The beheading of John the Baptist
At that time, Herod the tetrarch heard the news about Jesus,
So he said to his servants: "This is John the Baptist; he has risen from the dead! Therefore, the powers are at work through him."
For Herod had seized John, bound him, and put him in prison because of Herodias, the wife of his brother Philip,
Because John was telling him, "It is not lawful for you to have her."
And when he wanted to kill him, he feared the people, because he was like a prophet to them.
Then when Herod's birthday came, Herodias's daughter danced in the midst, and Herod was pleased.
He then promised under oath that whatever she asked for, he would give her.
So, having learned from her mother, she said: "Give me here on a platter the head of John the Baptist."
So the king was distressed. But for the sake of the oaths and those reclining with him, he ordered that it be given.
So he sent and beheaded John in prison.
So he brought his head on a platter and handed it to the boys, who brought it to his mother.
His disciples came forward, took up the body, and buried it. Then they came and told Jesus. The feeding of the five thousand men
When Jesus heard this, he withdrew from there in a boat to a solitary place. But the crowds heard about it and followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus went out, he saw a great crowd, had compassion on them, and healed their sick.
And when evening came, his disciples approached him, saying, "The place is deserted and the hour is already late. Send the crowds away so they can go to the villages and buy themselves food."
Then Jesus said to them, "They do not need to go away. You give them something to eat."
So they said to him: "We have here only five loaves and two fish."
He said, "Bring it to me here."
So he commanded the crowds to sit down on the grass. Then he took the five loaves and the two fish, looked up to heaven, blessed them, broke them, and gave the loaves to the disciples, and the disciples to the crowds.
So everyone ate and was satisfied. Then they lifted what was left of the fragments, twelve baskets full.
And the eaters were about five thousand men, besides women and children. The miracle of walking on water
At that time, Jesus insisted that his disciples get into the boat and go ahead of him to the other side, to dismiss the crowds.
After the crowds had dispersed, he went up the mountain alone to pray. And when evening came, he was there alone.
As for the ship, it had become in the middle of the sea, tormented by the waves. Because the wind was against it.
And in the fourth watch of the night, Jesus went to them, walking on the sea.
When the disciples saw him walking on the sea, they were troubled, saying, "It is a ghost." And they cried out in fear!
At once Jesus spoke to them, saying, "Take courage! It is I. Do not be afraid."
Then Peter answered him, "Lord, if it is you, command me to come to you on the water."
He said, "Come." So Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water to come to Jesus.
But when he saw the strong wind, he was afraid. And when he began to sink, he cried out, saying: "Lord, save me!"
Immediately Jesus reached out his hand and caught him, and said to him, "O you of little faith, why did you doubt?"
And when they entered the ship, the wind calmed.
And those in the ship came and prostrated to him, saying: "Truly, you are the Son of God!"
When they crossed over, they came to the land of Gennesaret,
The men of that place recognized him. So they sent to all the surrounding region and brought to him all the sick,
And they asked him to just touch the fringe of his garment. And all who touched him were healed.
The Pure and the Impure
Then came to Jesus scribes and Pharisees who were from Jerusalem, saying:
"Why do your students go beyond imitating the sheikhs, for they do not wash their hands when they eat bread?"
He answered and said to them: "And you also, why do you transgress the commandment of God because of your tradition?"
God has commanded, saying: Honor your father and your mother, and whoever insults a father or a mother, let him die a death.
And as for you, you say: Whoever says to his father or mother, "What you would have benefited from me is a gift devoted to God," he shall not honor his father or mother.
You have nullified the commandment of God because of your imitation!
O hypocrites! Isaiah has well prophesied about you, saying:
This people approach me with their mouth and honor me with their lips, but their heart is far from me.
And they worship me in vain while they know the teachings are the commandments of the people.
Then he called the assembly and said to them: "Listen and understand.
"It is not what enters the mouth that defiles a person, but what comes out of the mouth that defiles a person."
Then his disciples came and said to him, "Do you know that the Pharisees were offended when they heard this?"
He answered and said: "Every plant that my Heavenly Father did not plant will be uprooted."
Leave them. They are blind, blind leaders. And if a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Then Peter answered and said to him, "Explain this parable to us."
Then Jesus said, "Are you also still without understanding?"
Do you not yet understand that whatever enters the mouth goes into the stomach and is expelled?
As for what comes out of the mouth, it originates from the heart, and that defiles a person,
Because from the heart come evil thoughts: murder, adultery, immorality, theft, false testimony, blasphemy.
This is what defiles a person. As for eating with unwashed hands, it does not defile a person." The faith of the Canaanite woman
Then Jesus went out from there and withdrew to the regions of Tyre and Sidon.
And a Canaanite woman coming out from those borders cried to him, saying: "Have mercy on me, Lord, Son of David! My daughter is very much possessed."
So he did not answer her a word. Then his disciples came and asked him, saying, "Send her away, because she is crying out after us!"
He answered and said, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel."
So she came and prostrated before him, saying: "O master, help me!"
He answered and said, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
She said: "Yes, sir! And dogs also eat from the scraps that fall from their masters' tables!"
Then Jesus answered her, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be done for you as you wish." And her daughter was healed from that hour. Feeding the four thousand men
Then Jesus went from there and came to the side of the Sea of Galilee, and he went up the mountain and sat there.
Then large crowds came to him, bringing with them the lame, the blind, the mute, the crippled, and many others, and they laid them at Jesus' feet. And he healed them.
So that the crowds were amazed when they saw the mute speaking, the lame walking, and the blind seeing. And they glorified the God of Israel.
As for Jesus, he called his disciples and said, "I have compassion on the crowd, because they have been with me for three days now and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, lest they faint on the way."
So his disciples said to him, "Where can we get enough bread in the wilderness to satisfy such a large crowd?"
Then Jesus said to them, "How many loaves do you have?" They said, "Seven, and a few small fish."
So the crowds were commanded to recline on the ground,
And he took the seven loaves and the fish, gave thanks, broke them, and gave them to his disciples, and the disciples gave them to the crowd.
So everyone ate and was satisfied. Then they raised what was left of the fragments, seven baskets full,
And the eaters were four thousand men, besides the women and the children.
Then he dismissed the crowds, boarded the ship, and came to the outskirts of Magdala.
Request for a verse
Then the Pharisees and the Sadducees came to him to test him, and they asked him to show them a sign from heaven.
He answered and said to them: "If it is evening, you say: Clear, because the sky is red.
And in the morning: today is winter because the sky is red with a frown. O hypocrites! You know how to distinguish the face of the sky, but you cannot discern the signs of the times!
An evil, wicked generation seeks a sign, but no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah the prophet." Then he left them and went away. The yeast of the Pharisees and the Sadducees.
And when his disciples came to the Hebrews, they forgot to take bread.
And Jesus said to them, "Watch out and beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
So they thought to themselves, saying: "We have not taken any bread."
Then Jesus knew and said to them, "Why do you think within yourselves, O you of little faith, that you have not brought any bread?
Do you still not understand? And do you not remember the five loaves, the five thousand, and how many baskets you took?
And not seven loaves, the four thousands, and how many baskets did you take?
How do you not understand that I was not talking about bread? I told you to beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and the Sadducees.
Then they understood that he did not say to beware of the yeast of bread, but of the teaching of the Pharisees and the Sadducees. Peter's confession of Christ
When Jesus came to the region of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, "Who do people say that the Son of Man is?"
They said: "Some say John the Baptist, others say Elijah, and others say Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
He said to them, "But who do you say that I am?"
Simon Peter answered, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God!"
And Jesus answered him, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven.
And I also say to you: You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.
And I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven; whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then he instructed his disciples not to tell anyone that he was Jesus the Messiah. Jesus foretells his death and resurrection.
From that time Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders, chief priests, and scribes, and be killed, and on the third day rise again.
But Peter took him aside and began to rebuke him, saying, "Far be it from you, Lord! This shall never happen to you!"
Then he turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me, because you do not have in mind the things of God, but the things of men."
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "If anyone wants to come after me, let him deny himself, take up his cross, and follow me,
For whoever wants to save their life will lose it, but whoever loses their life for my sake will find it.
For what will it profit a man if he gains the whole world and loses his own soul? Or what will a man give in exchange for his soul?
For the Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and then He will reward each one according to his works.
Truly I tell you: There are some standing here who will not taste death until they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
The Manifestation
And after six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John his brother, and led them up a high mountain by themselves.
And his appearance was altered before them, and his face shone like the sun, and his clothes became white like light.
And Moses and Elijah appeared to them, talking with him.
Then Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here! If you wish, I will make three shelters here: one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And while he was speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice came from the cloud saying: "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to Him."
And when the disciples heard it, they fell on their faces and were very afraid.
Then Jesus came and touched them, saying, "Get up, and do not be afraid."
So they lifted up their eyes and saw no one except Jesus alone.
And as they were coming down from the mountain, Jesus commanded them, saying, "Tell no one what you have seen until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
And his disciples asked him, saying, "Why then do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
And Jesus answered them, saying, "Indeed, Elijah comes first and restores all things."
But I tell you: Elijah has come, and they did not recognize him, but did to him whatever they wished. Likewise, the Son of Man will also suffer at their hands.
Then the disciples understood that he was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Healing a boy possessed by a demon.
And when they came to the assembly, a man came forward to him kneeling.
Saying: "O master, have mercy on my son, for he is struck down and suffers greatly, and he often falls into the fire and often into the water."
And I brought him to your disciples, but they were not able to heal him."
Jesus answered, "O unbelieving and twisted generation, how long shall I be with you? How long shall I endure you? Bring him here!"
Then Jesus rebuked him, and the demon came out of him. And the boy was healed from that hour.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and said, "Why were we not able to cast it out?"
Then Jesus said to them, "Because of your unbelief. Truly I tell you, if you have faith as small as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move; nothing will be impossible for you.
As for this kind, it is only removed by prayer and fasting.
While they were hesitating in Galilee, Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
"They kill him, and on the third day he rises." So they were very sad. Temple tax
And when they came to Capernaum, those who collected the two-drachma tax approached Peter and said, "Does your teacher not pay the two-drachma tax?"
He said, "Yes." When he entered the house, Jesus went ahead of him and said, "What do you think, Simon? From whom do the kings of the earth take toll or tribute, from their own children or from strangers?"
Peter said to him, "From the foreigners." Jesus said to him, "But the children are free."
But so that we do not stumble upon them, go to the sea and cast a fishing line, and take the first fish that comes up, and when it opens its mouth you will find a scroll, so take it and give it to them on my behalf and yours."
